  "summary": "### Summary",
  "details": "### Summary\r\n\r\nZITADEL’s hosted **Login V1** UI allowed an unauthenticated attacker who knows only a victim’s login name to permanently change that account’s username. By starting an ordinary login flow, submitting the victim’s login name to identify the account, and then posting a new username, the attacker could rename any active human user on the instance — including administrators — without ever supplying a password or any second factor.\r\n\r\nThe change-username request acted on the account bound to the login flow as soon as a login name had been entered, before the flow had reached the change-username step and before any authentication factor had been verified.\r\n\r\n### Impact\r\n\r\nAn attacker with only network access to the hosted **Login V1** UI and knowledge of a victim’s login name can:\r\n\r\n- **Rename any active human account** on the instance, across every organization, including instance administrators\r\n- **Lock the victim out** of their account, because the identifier they previously logged in with no longer resolves to them\r\n- **Free the victim’s former login name for re-registration**, where the organization’s login policy permits self-registration or domain discovery, enabling squatting of the released identifier\r\n\r\nThis affects deployments that authenticate users through ZITADEL’s hosted **Login V1** UI (OIDC/SAML).\r\n\r\nOn its own this issue does **not** grant account takeover: no password, session, or token is disclosed, and the attacker cannot authenticate as the victim. The impact is to **integrity** (unauthorized modification of account identifiers) and **availability** (lockout of the legitimate owner). **Machine and service accounts are not affected**, because the login-name step binds only active human users, so a non-human account cannot be selected as the rename target through this flow. The standalone **Login V2** application does not expose this change-username flow and is not vulnerable through it.\r\n\r\n**Scope note:** This is an instance-wide, cross-organization flaw on the hosted **Login V1** surface: any active human in any organization on the instance can be renamed by an anonymous caller. It does not cross instance boundaries. It shares its root cause with `GHSA-72q5-mv5c-vxv4` — a **Login V1** flow handler that acted on the account bound by login name before the flow had reached the relevant step or verified a factor, fixed the same way by gating the handler on the flow’s current step. That advisory covered unauthenticated MFA enrollment and phone-number overwrite; this one covers the username change.\r\n\r\n### Affected Versions\r\n\r\nSystems running one of the following versions are affected:\r\n\r\n* **4.x:** `4.0.0` through `4.17.3` (including RC versions)\r\n* **3.x:** `3.0.0` through `3.4.15` (including RC versions)\r\n\r\n### Patches\r\n\r\nThe vulnerability has been addressed in the latest releases. The hosted **Login V1** UI now performs a username change only once the login flow has legitimately reached the change-username step, which is presented only after the first factor and any required multi-factor checks have succeeded. A request that has merely identified an account by login name can no longer trigger a rename.\r\n\r\n* **4.x**: Upgrade to $\\ge$ [`v4.19.1`](https://github.com/zitadel/zitadel/releases/tag/v4.19.1)\r\n\r\n⚠️ Please skip the v4.18.0 release and use v4.19.1. The setup step fails in this version (https://github.com/zitadel/zitadel/issues/12780).\r\n\r\nThe **3.x** release line reached end-of-life on 2026-08-31 and will not receive a fix for this vulnerability. Affected 3.x deployments must upgrade to a patched 4.x release.\r\n\r\n### Workarounds\r\n\r\nThe recommended solution is to upgrade to a patched version. There is no configuration that fully closes this gap on unpatched versions.\r\n\r\n### Questions\r\n\r\nIf you have any questions or comments about this advisory, please email us at security@zitadel.com\r\n\r\n### Credits\r\n\r\nThanks to Adam Korczynski (Ada Logics) for reporting the vulnerability.",
